   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to the\nsuspension or revocation of fishing, hunting and trapping licenses pursuant to\nthe interstate wildlife violator compact",
    "basePrintNo": "S2413",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S2413-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §71-0919, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S2413\n\nSPONSOR: KAPLAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to the\nsuspension or revocation of fishing, hunting and trapping licenses\npursuant to the interstate wildlife violator compact\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nEnvironmental Conservation Law (ECL)§ 71-0919(1) is amended-by adding a\nnew paragraph g to provide that when a license privilege is revoked or\nsuspended by a participating state in the IWVC, the violation and subse-\nquent revocation or suspension in that state shall constitute the basis\nfor revocation or suspension in this state for the same privilege and\nperiod of time.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nTitle 25 of ECL Article 11 enacted the IWVC. ECL § 71-0919 sets for the\npunishments for violations of the Fish and Wildlife Law\n\n \nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nThe text of the IWVC is found at ECL § 11-2503. Article V of the IWVC\nprovides reciprocal recognition of suspensions requiring that all\nparticipating states recognize the suspension of license privileges of\nany person by any participating state as though the violation resulting\nin the suspension had occurred in their state and could have been the\nbasis for suspension of license privileges in their state. Each partic-\nipating state is responsible for communicating suspension information to\nthe other participating states.\n\nNew York State-joined the IWVC in 2005; at that time there were 16\nparticipating states in the Compact, today there are 30. Currently, each\nrevocation notice posted on the IWVC Web page must be painstakingly\nreviewed for content and applicability to New York revocable violations.\nOther participating states eliminate this large administrative burden by\nsimply adopting all revocations and suspensions posted by other partic-\nipating states.\n\nThe purpose of the IWVC is to prevent offenders from farming around for\nsporting privileges when they have been revoked in another state, it is\nalso intended to prevent member States from becoming a dumping ground\nfor all the violators that can't get a sporting license any where else.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n3185 of 2018, Passed Assembly.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPACT:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
